Koufu Food Co., Ltd. v. Industrial Bank of Korea, Bank of China, Lianyungang Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch — Dispute over Letter of Credit

[Ruling Summary]

Letter of credit fraud refers to conduct in which the beneficiary of a letter of credit, when there is no goods at all or when the goods are of inferior quality and cannot be delivered, unilaterally or in malicious collusion with others, forges one or more types of documents that conform to the requirements of the letter of credit, thereby defrauding the issuing bank of the proceeds under the letter of credit and causing economic loss to the applicant. If the issuing bank has no evidence that the documents under the letter of credit were forged by the beneficiary unilaterally or in malicious collusion with others for the purpose of defrauding the issuing bank of the proceeds under the letter of credit, and such forgery has caused substantial harm to the applicant, the issuing bank cannot invoke the fraud exception principle to refuse payment under the letter of credit.

Plaintiff: Lianyungang Koufu Food Co., Ltd., domiciled at Lianyun District, Lianyungang City, Jiangsu Province.

Legal Representative: Weng Jun, Chairman of the Board of the company.

Defendant: Industrial Bank of Korea (Seoul Head Office), domiciled at Jung-gu, Seoul, South Korea.

Legal Representative: Kim Jong-chang, Chairman of the Board of the bank.

Defendant: Bank of China, Lianyungang Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch, domiciled at Lianyun District, Lianyungang City, Jiangsu Province.

Responsible Person: Lou Peiyun, Branch Manager.

The plaintiff, Lianyungang Koufu Food Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Koufu Food Company), filed a lawsuit with the Nanjing Intermediate People's Court of Jiangsu Province against the defendants, Industrial Bank of Korea Seoul Head Office (hereinafter referred to as Industrial Bank of Korea) and Bank of China, Lianyungang Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch (hereinafter referred to as BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch), over a dispute concerning a letter of credit.

Plaintiff's Allegations: As the beneficiary of an irrevocable letter of credit issued by the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea, the plaintiff had timely loaded the goods on board the vessel in accordance with the requirements of the letter of credit and submitted to the defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch the full set of documents under the letter of credit. After examination, the BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch confirmed that the documents were consistent with the terms of the letter of credit and among themselves, and then sent the full set of documents to Industrial Bank of Korea. However, Industrial Bank of Korea unreasonably refused to pay. This refusal by Industrial Bank of Korea violates the provisions of the *Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits* (International Chamber of Commerce Publication No. 500, hereinafter UCP500). Therefore, the plaintiff requests that the court order Industrial Bank of Korea to fulfill its obligations as the issuing bank, pay the plaintiff USD 110,500 in proceeds under the letter of credit plus corresponding interest, and bear the litigation costs. As the negotiating bank, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch shall bear joint and several liability for this.

The plaintiff, Koufu Food Company, submitted the following evidence:

1. An irrevocable letter of credit with number M04E5204NS00484 and its Chinese translation, to prove the existence of a legal relationship under the letter of credit among the parties;
2. The full set of documents under the said letter of credit and their Chinese translation, to prove that Koufu Food Company had submitted the full set of documents as required;
3. A letter of refusal of payment and its Chinese translation, to prove that the proceeds under the letter of credit had been refused by Industrial Bank of Korea;
4. A lawyer's letter, to prove that Koufu Food Company could not accept the reasons for refusal put forward by Industrial Bank of Korea;
5. Three responses to the letter of refusal and their Chinese translation, to prove that BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch also believed that the reasons for refusal raised by Industrial Bank of Korea were unfounded;
6. A covering letter for the documentary draft, to prove that BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch, in the name of the negotiating bank, had requested the issuing bank, Industrial Bank of Korea, to reimburse the proceeds under the letter of credit;
7. The documentary draft and its Chinese translation, to prove that BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch had endorsed the draft and delivered it to the issuing bank, thereby exercising its rights under the instrument in the name of the negotiating bank;
8. Receipts for document examination fees and postage, to prove that BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch had examined the full set of documents submitted by Koufu Food Company and had accordingly charged document examination fees;
9. The fee schedule for international settlement business of Bank of China, to prove that BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch charged fees according to the standards for a negotiating bank, and therefore should bear the responsibilities of a negotiating bank.

Defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch defended as follows: In the letter of credit relationship in this case, this defendant only handled the documents submitted by the plaintiff for onward transmission. It was a remitting bank, not a negotiating bank. The plaintiff's claim that this defendant should bear joint and several liability on the ground that it was the negotiating bank of the letter of credit lacks factual basis and legal support.

Defendant Industrial Bank of Korea did not submit evidence within the time limit for evidence submission set by the court, nor did it appear in court at the first instance. In its written defense submitted after the hearing, it argued: Since China has no letter of credit law, this defendant makes its defense in accordance with the UCP500. There are discrepancies in the documents submitted by the plaintiff under the letter of credit, and there are also fraudulent acts such as backdated bills of lading and forged documents. In accordance with the international practice of the fraud exception, this defendant is entitled to refuse payment of the proceeds under the letter of credit.

Defendant Industrial Bank of Korea submitted the following evidence and requested the court to organize cross-examination:

1. The irrevocable letter of credit numbered M04E5204NS00484 and the full set of documents and drafts under that letter of credit, to prove that the remitting bank indicated by Koufu Food Company on the draft was "INDUSTRIAL BANK OF KOREA SEOUL (HEAD OFFICE SEOUL)", which is inconsistent with the name of the remitting bank on the letter of credit, "INDUSTRIAL BANK OF KOREA (HEAD OFFICE SEOUL) SEOUL"; that the description of the goods in each document is inconsistent; that the packing list contains a unit of weight while the invoice does not, and other discrepancies;
2. A copy of the duplicate bill of lading issued by COSCO Container Lines Co., Ltd. (hereinafter COSCO), to prove that the actual date of loading of the goods in question was June 1, 2002, while the bill of lading submitted by Koufu Food Company under the letter of credit had backdated the loading date to May 31, 2002;
3. The business registration materials of Koufu Food Company, to prove that the correct English name of Koufu Food Company is "LIANYUNGANG KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD.", but on the negotiating documents under the letter of credit submitted by Koufu Food Company, it was written as "LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD.";
4. The sales contract under the letter of credit, to prove that the seal of Koufu Food Company is inconsistent with the seal used on the negotiating documents it submitted;
5. Documents under another letter of credit with Koufu Food Company as beneficiary, to prove that the name and seal previously used by Koufu Food Company differ from those under the letter of credit in this case, and therefore the documents and seal under this letter of credit were forged by Koufu Food Company.

Both the plaintiff Koufu Food Company and the defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch argued that since the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea submitted its defense and evidence only after the statutory time limits for defense and evidence submission had expired, it should be deemed to have waived its rights to defend and present evidence, and therefore they did not cross-examine the evidence submitted by it. At the same time, Koufu Food Company further explained: (1) In the letter of credit issued by Industrial Bank of Korea, the name of the remitting bank appeared in three places, two of which used "INDUSTRIAL BANK OF KOREA SEOUL (HEAD OFFICE SEOUL)", which is consistent with the name of the remitting bank on our company's draft; only one place used "INDUSTRIAL BANK OF KOREA (HEAD OFFICE SEOUL) SEOUL". Moreover, Industrial Bank of Korea itself, in its own written defense, also used the same name of the remitting bank as that on our company's draft. Therefore, even if there was an error in the name of the remitting bank, it was caused by Industrial Bank of Korea. (2) According to Chinese law, the name of a Chinese enterprise is based on the Chinese name registered with the administrative authority for industry and commerce; the foreign language name is not a statutory name. Therefore, it is not illegal for our company to mistakenly write the letter "G" as "D" in its foreign language name. (3) Industrial Bank of Korea attempted to prove that our company backdated the bill of lading by submitting a copy of the duplicate bill of lading after the hearing, but the content of this copy is inconsistent with the original bill of lading submitted by both parties to the court; moreover, the source of the copy is unclear, and as evidence it has formal defects, and cannot prove the backdating issue.

The Nanjing Intermediate People's Court found the following facts upon trial:

On April 24, 2002, upon the application of Seoul Changji Enterprise Co., Ltd. (hereinafter Changji Company), the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea issued an irrevocable documentary letter of credit numbered M04E5204NS00484, with a total amount of USD 110,500, valid until June 30 of the same year. The English name of the beneficiary indicated on the letter of credit was LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD. The negotiating bank was any bank. The method of payment was sight payment. The drawee was Industrial Bank of Korea. The latest shipment date was May 31, 2002. The required documents were: signed commercial invoice in triplicate, full set of original clean bills of lading, and packing list in triplicate. The letter of credit also stipulated the period for presentation of documents, negotiation, etc. After receiving the letter of credit, the plaintiff Koufu Food Company, on June 6, 2002, submitted the full set of documents under the letter of credit to the defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch. The original bill of lading indicated the date of loading as May 31, 2002. After receiving the documents, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch conducted a strict examination and, on June 7 of the same month, sent them by courier to the issuing bank. On June 19, 2002, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch received two notices of refusal of payment from Industrial Bank of Korea. The reasons for refusal were: (1) The descriptions of the goods on the invoice, packing list, and bill of lading were inconsistent; (2) the date of loading on the bill of lading was forged; (3) the name of the remitting bank on the draft was inconsistent with that on the letter of credit; (4) the address of the consignee was not indicated. After receiving the notice of refusal, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch replied to Industrial Bank of Korea on June 20, pointing out that the alleged discrepancies did not exist and requesting it to accept the full set of documents and make immediate payment. On June 26, Industrial Bank of Korea sent a second letter to BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch, no longer raising the issue of discrepancies, but stating: "The applicant informed our bank that they had previously notified your bank of the fraud and warned your bank not to accept the beneficiary's documents. Currently, the applicant is suing the beneficiary for the fraud. Our bank has evidence that the documents are forged and that the fraud is ongoing." Thereafter, although BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch repeatedly communicated with Industrial Bank of Korea, requesting it to fulfill its payment obligation as the issuing bank, it never received a reply from Industrial Bank of Korea. On September 3, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch received from Industrial Bank of Korea the returned documents along with a copy of a stop-payment order issued by a Seoul District Court in South Korea. On September 9, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch returned the documents to Koufu Food Company, which then filed the present lawsuit.

The Nanjing Intermediate People's Court held:

Article 34, paragraph (1) of the Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on Evidence in Civil Procedures states: "A party shall submit evidentiary materials to the people's court within the time limit for submission of evidence. If a party fails to submit evidence within the time limit, it shall be deemed to have waived its right to present evidence." Paragraph (2) states: "For evidentiary materials submitted by a party beyond the time limit, the people's court shall not organize cross-examination during trial, unless the opposing party agrees to cross-examination." Article 43 states: "Evidence provided after the expiration of the time limit for submission of evidence that is not new evidence shall not be admitted by the people's court." "If the people's court has granted a party an extension of the time limit for submission of evidence, but the party, due to objective reasons, fails to provide the evidence within the extended time limit, and not hearing such evidence may lead to a manifestly unjust judgment, the evidence provided may be deemed as new evidence." Within the prescribed time limit for evidence submission, the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea failed to submit evidence and did not apply for an extension. The evidence it submitted after the hearing does not constitute newly discovered evidence after the expiration of the time limit, nor evidence that could not be provided within the time limit. Since the other parties in this case refused to cross-examine, the court will not organize cross-examination of the evidence submitted by Industrial Bank of Korea.

Article 142, paragraph (3) of the General Principles of the Civil Law of the People's Republic of China (hereinafter the General Principles of Civil Law) provides: "If there is no provision in the laws of the People's Republic of China or in the international treaties concluded or acceded to by the People's Republic of China, international practice may be applied." In this case, the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea is a foreign legal person, and the cause of action is a foreign-related letter of credit dispute. In their statements of claim and defense, all parties relied on the UCP500. Since China currently has no legal provisions governing letter of credit relationships, and the UCP500 is the international practice regulating such relationships, it shall be applied in this case.

Article 9(a)(i) of the UCP500 provides that for an irrevocable credit available by sight payment, if the stipulated documents are presented to the nominated bank or the issuing bank and that they comply with the terms and conditions of the credit, this constitutes a definite undertaking of the issuing bank to effect payment at sight. As the issuing bank, the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea issued an irrevocable letter of credit payable at sight. As long as the plaintiff Koufu Food Company presented the documents to the defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch in accordance with the terms of the letter of credit, and such documents were forwarded by BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch to Industrial Bank of Korea, Industrial Bank of Korea was obligated to fulfill its duty as the issuing bank and pay the proceeds under the letter of credit to Koufu Food Company. Article 14(b) of the UCP500 provides: "Upon receipt of the documents, the issuing bank and/or confirming bank, if any, or a nominated bank acting on their behalf, must determine on the basis of the documents alone whether they appear on their face to be in compliance with the credit terms and conditions. If the documents appear not to be in compliance with the credit terms and conditions, the banks may refuse to take up the documents." Article 14(d)(i) provides: "If the issuing bank and/or confirming bank, if any, or a nominated bank acting on their behalf, decides to refuse the documents, it must give notice to that effect by telecommunication or, if that is not possible, by other expeditious means, without delay but no later than the close of the seventh banking day following the day of receipt of the documents. Such notice shall be given to the bank from which it received the documents, or, if it received the documents directly from the beneficiary, to the beneficiary." After receiving the documents forwarded by BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch, Industrial Bank of Korea initially raised document discrepancies. However, after BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch replied stating that the discrepancies were not established, Industrial Bank of Korea, within the period for valid notice under the UCP500, did not raise the issue of discrepancies again and thus lost the right to refuse to take up the documents on grounds of document discrepancies. Industrial Bank of Korea eventually returned the documents on the grounds of letter of credit fraud and the stop-payment order issued by the Seoul District Court in South Korea. After Koufu Food Company filed the lawsuit, Industrial Bank of Korea again defended on the grounds of document discrepancies, which is clearly inconsistent with the facts and the UCP500. Article 3(a) of the UCP500 provides: "Credits, by their nature, are separate transactions from the sales contracts or other contracts on which they may be based. Banks are in no way concerned with or bound by such contracts, even if any reference whatsoever to such contracts is included in the credit. Consequently, the undertaking of a bank to pay, accept and pay drafts or negotiate and/or to fulfill any other obligation under the credit is not subject to claims or defenses by the applicant resulting from its relationships with the issuing bank or the beneficiary." The relationship under a letter of credit is independent of the underlying contract and is not subject to claims or defenses arising from the underlying contract relationship. Although fraud may constitute an exception, Industrial Bank of Korea failed to submit any evidence sufficient to prove the existence of fraud within the time limit for evidence submission, and therefore this defense also cannot be sustained. Under these circumstances, Koufu Food Company's request for Industrial Bank of Korea to pay the proceeds under the letter of credit and corresponding interest should be supported.

The plaintiff Koufu Food Company and the defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch did not enter into a written agreement regarding negotiation. There is no contractual relationship concerning negotiation between them. BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch has no contractual obligation to negotiate the documents. Article 10(b)(ii) of the UCP500 provides: "Negotiation means the giving of value for drafts and/or documents by the bank authorized to negotiate. Mere examination of the documents without giving of value does not constitute a negotiation." Article 10(c) provides: "Unless the nominated bank is the confirming bank, nomination by the issuing bank does not constitute any undertaking by the nominated bank to pay, incur a deferred payment undertaking, accept drafts or negotiate. The nominated bank's receipt and/or examination and/or forwarding of documents does not make that bank liable to pay, incur a deferred payment undertaking, accept drafts or negotiate, unless it has expressly agreed to do so and so advised the beneficiary." BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch is not a confirming bank under the letter of credit. Although the letter of credit allowed any bank to be the negotiating bank, BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch did not expressly agree to negotiate after receiving the letter of credit and the full set of documents from Koufu Food Company, nor did it give value. It only handled the documents by examining and forwarding them. Therefore, this sub-branch is a remitting bank, not a negotiating bank as defined in the UCP500, and has no statutory obligation to negotiate. Koufu Food Company's request that BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch bear joint and several liability for payment, on the ground that it is a negotiating bank, lacks factual basis and legal support and cannot be sustained.

Accordingly, the Nanjing Intermediate People's Court rendered the following judgment on December 30, 2002:

1. The defendant Industrial Bank of Korea shall, within 10 days after this judgment takes effect, pay the plaintiff Koufu Food Company the proceeds under the letter of credit, in the amount of USD 110,500, together with interest;

2. The plaintiff's claim against the defendant BOC Nuclear Power Station Sub-branch is dismissed.

Litigation costs in the amount of RMB 14,137 shall be borne by the defendant Industrial Bank of Korea.

Industrial Bank of Korea, dissatisfied with the first-instance judgment, appealed to the Jiangsu High People's Court. The grounds for appeal were: (1) The beneficiary of the letter of credit in this case is LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD., while the appellee's English name is LIANYUNGANG KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD. Therefore, Koufu Food Company is not the beneficiary of this letter of credit. The first-instance judgment made an error in determining the subject. (2) Koufu Food Company privately engraved a seal, forged documents that were not under its own name, and backdated the date of shipment on the bill of lading. These fraudulent acts against the parties under the letter of credit are well established by evidence. (3) The true motive behind Koufu Food Company's fraudulent conduct was that the goods it shipped were of inferior quality, and it could only resort to these means to deceive the appellant in order to obtain the proceeds under the letter of credit by presenting forged documents. In summary, Koufu Food Company breached the trade contract first, then forged the negotiating documents to defraud the appellant when trying to negotiate the letter of credit. Moreover, because the goods arrived at the port late, substantial harm was caused to the applicant. In accordance with the international practice of the letter of credit fraud exception, the appellant is entitled to refuse payment under the letter of credit. The appellant requests that the judgment on appeal be reversed and the claims of Koufu Food Company be dismissed.

Industrial Bank of Korea submitted the following evidence to the court of second instance:

1. The articles of association of Koufu Food Company, to prove that the English name registered with the administrative authority for industry and commerce is "LIANYUNGANG KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD.", not "LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS CO., LTD." as shown on the letter of credit negotiating documents;

2. The negotiating documents submitted by Koufuku Foods Company to Korea Exchange Bank in other transactions, used to prove that Koufuku Foods Company had previously engaged in fraudulent conduct and used different names and seals from those in the present negotiation;

3. The complete set of documents under the L/C in this case and their Chinese translations, used to prove that the English name used by Koufuku Foods Company when requesting payment was inconsistent with its correct English name;

4. Copies each of the May and June 2002 sailing schedules published by Qingdao Haifa Industrial Company, used to prove that the sailing date of the vessel "Lingquan River" involved in this case was June 1, and that the date filled in by Koufuku Foods Company on the bill of lading was backdated.

In addition to submitting the above evidence, Korea Exchange Bank also applied to the court of second instance to seal the navigation logs of the "Lingquan River" vessel owned by COSCO for May to June 2002 to verify the actual loading date of the goods under the bill of lading involved in this case, on the ground that it was unable to obtain a copy of the bill of lading dated June 1, 2002, for objective reasons.

Koufuku Foods Company argued in its defense: 1. Evidence 1 to 3 submitted by the appellant Korea Exchange Bank in the second instance were consistent with the evidence submitted by it to the court of first instance after the trial of the first instance and were overdue evidence, which could not, as a matter of law, serve as the basis for deciding the case; 2. All negotiating documents presented by Koufuku Foods Company to Korea Exchange Bank complied with the provisions of UCP500, and there was no fraudulent conduct whatsoever; Korea Exchange Bank should bear the unconditional obligation to pay; 3. Koufuku Foods Company had loaded the goods within the period stipulated in the L/C, and there was no conduct of backdating the bill of lading. If Korea Exchange Bank believed that the seller had used the contract to commit fraud, it must provide sufficient evidence to support its claim; 4. Korea Exchange Bank's refusal to pay the L/C on contradictory grounds did not conform to international trade payment practices. The real reason for its refusal to pay was that the importer was unable to pay for and take delivery of the documents. The grounds of appeal of Korea Exchange Bank were untenable, and the judgment of the first instance should be affirmed.

China Bank Nuclear Power Plant Sub-branch argued in its defense: The judgment of the first instance concerning this Sub-branch was correct and should be affirmed.

Upon application by Korea Exchange Bank and in light of the actual circumstances of this case, the Jiangsu High People's Court, ex officio, obtained the following evidence:

1. A letter from COSCO to Qingdao COSCO International Freight Co., Ltd. (the port company of COSCO in Qingdao), the main content being: COSCO required Qingdao COSCO International Freight Co., Ltd. to complete loading by May 31, 2002, in accordance with the latest loading date specified by Koufuku Foods Company, and to issue to Koufuku Foods Company a shipped-on-board bill of lading dated May 31;

2. The cargo loading list for the goods involved in this case obtained from China Ocean Shipping Tally Company Qingdao Branch, the main content being: Koufuku Foods Company had delivered the goods to the station designated by Qingdao COSCO International Freight Co., Ltd. by May 31, and Customs had also released the goods on May 31;

3. The loading operation sheets for the goods involved in this case obtained from China Ocean Shipping Tally Company Qingdao Branch, the main content being: The loading time of the goods involved in this case onto the "Lingquan River" vessel was from 8:00 on May 31, 2002, to 4:00 on June 1, 2002;

4. A printed copy of the bill of lading for the goods involved in this case obtained from Qingdao COSCO International Freight Co., Ltd., confirming that the bill of lading was issued on June 1, 2002;

5. A record of investigation of Jin'an Branch of Sinotrans Shandong Co., Ltd., the main content being: Entrusted by Koufuku Foods Company, in handling matters such as booking space, customs declaration, and wharf cargo receipt, this company first contacted Pan-Ocean Shipping Co., Ltd. to carry the goods involved in this case, but was refused, and then contacted COSCO, which promised to load the goods involved in this case before 24:00 on May 31;

6. A record of investigation of Qingdao COSCO International Freight Co., Ltd., the main content being: Upon instruction from Jiang Yunlong of COSCO Container Lines Co., Ltd., this company began loading the goods involved in this case on May 31; as some goods were loaded in the early morning hours of the next day, the bill of lading was issued on June 1.

All parties acknowledged the above evidence obtained by the court.

The Jiangsu High People's Court, upon trial, confirmed the facts found in the first instance and additionally found the following:

After receiving the L/C issued by the appellant Korea Exchange Bank, the appellee Koufuku Foods Company organized the goods under the L/C and contacted Korea Pan-Ocean Shipping Co., Ltd. for shipment; upon refusal, it contacted COSCO, which promised to load the goods before the latest loading date specified in the L/C. From 8:00 on May 31, 2002, to 4:00 on June 1, 2002, the goods under the L/C in this case were loaded onto the "Lingquan River" vessel; on June 1, the "Lingquan River" vessel sailed; on the same day, the carrier issued the bill of lading for the goods, but on the bill of lading issued to Koufuku Foods Company, the carrier filled in the loading time as May 31, 2002.

In the L/C issued by the appellant Korea Exchange Bank, the beneficiary was named as "LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS COLTD." To make the negotiating documents consistent with the L/C, the appellee Koufuku Foods Company changed the English name from "LIANYUNGANG" to "LIANYUNGAND" and affixed a seal containing the English wording "LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS COLTD" on the L/C negotiating documents.

In addition, although the appellant Korea Exchange Bank claimed that the goods supplied by the appellee Koufuku Foods Company were of inferior quality and that the delayed arrival of the goods had caused substantial damage to the L/C applicant, it did not submit corresponding evidence. Regarding the carrier's backdating of the bill of lading, at the trial of the second instance, Korea Exchange Bank also failed to submit evidence that the carrier was instructed by Koufuku Foods Company or had maliciously colluded with Koufuku Foods Company. At the trial of the second instance, Korea Exchange Bank indicated that it was abandoning the first ground of appeal, i.e., it no longer asserted that Koufuku Foods Company was not the beneficiary of the L/C in this case.

The issues to be resolved in the second instance were: 1. The issue of applicable law; 2. The issue of whether fraudulent conduct constituted by forging documents; 3. The issue of whether fraudulent conduct constituted by backdating the bill of lading.

The Jiangsu High People's Court held:

Concerning the first issue. This case is a dispute over an L/C transaction. In the first instance, each party relied on UCP500 as the basis for their claims and defenses, and the first instance also applied UCP500 to render its judgment. However, UCP500 can only resolve the status and rights and obligations of the parties in an L/C transaction; it does not address L/C fraud and its legal remedies, and therefore cannot resolve the issue of L/C fraud raised by the appellant Korea Exchange Bank. On this issue, Korea Exchange Bank argued that Korean law was the governing law, while the appellee Koufuku Foods Company and the original defendant China Bank Nuclear Power Plant Sub-branch contended that Chinese law should be the governing law. L/C fraud is a tort. Article 146, paragraph 1 of the General Principles of Civil Law provides: "The compensation for damages caused by a tort shall be governed by the law of the place where the tort was committed." Since Korea Exchange Bank claimed that Koufuku Foods Company had forged documents and backdated the bill of lading, and the documents and bill of lading under the L/C in this case were all issued in China, China is the place where the tort was committed. Therefore, Chinese law should be applied to resolve the issues of L/C fraud and its legal remedies.

Concerning the second issue. L/C fraud refers to conduct where the L/C beneficiary, having no goods or being unable to deliver goods of inferior quality, alone or in malicious collusion with others, forges one or more types of documents that comply with the L/C requirements to obtain payment under the L/C from the issuing bank, thereby causing economic loss to the L/C applicant. In this case, the appellee Koufuku Foods Company had delivered the goods to the carrier, and then prepared or obtained the commercial invoice, draft, packing list, bill of lading, and other documents required by the L/C. The appellant Korea Exchange Bank had no evidence to prove that the goods supplied by Koufuku Foods Company were of inferior quality. Therefore, there was no issue of Koufuku Foods Company using inferior-quality goods to defraud payment under the L/C. Given that Korea Exchange Bank had incorrectly written the English name of the beneficiary Koufuku Foods Company as "LIANYUNGAND KUCHIFUKU FOODS COLTD" in the L/C it issued, to make the negotiating documents consistent with the L/C, Koufuku Foods Company filled in the English name of the company in the L/C negotiating documents as the same incorrect name, consistent with the L/C, and simultaneously affixed a seal containing the same English name. Although the seal affixed by Koufuku Foods Company on the L/C negotiating documents contained the incorrect English name, it also contained the correct Chinese name of the company. At the trial of the second instance, Korea Exchange Bank no longer disputed Koufuku Foods Company's status as the L/C beneficiary, indicating that the use of the term "LIANYUNGAND" by Koufuku Foods Company in the L/C negotiating documents objectively did not cause any ambiguity. Therefore, the incorrect filling of the company's English name in the L/C negotiating documents and the affixing of a seal containing the same incorrect English name by Koufuku Foods Company was justified and did not constitute L/C fraud. The ground of appeal of Korea Exchange Bank that Koufuku Foods Company had privately engraved a seal, forged documents, and constituted L/C fraud was untenable.

Concerning the third issue. The existing evidence proves that the goods involved in this case were loaded from 8:00 on May 31, 2002, to 4:00 on June 1, 2002, and the carrier issued the bill of lading on June 1, while the loading time filled in on the bill of lading issued by the carrier to the appellee Koufuku Foods Company was May 31, 2002, which is indeed a backdate. Even so, it cannot be determined that Koufuku Foods Company engaged in L/C fraud. This is because: 1. Before the loading date stipulated in the L/C, Koufuku Foods Company had already organized the goods, delivered them to the station designated by the carrier, fulfilled necessary procedures such as customs clearance, and obtained the carrier's promise of loading on May 31; objectively, there was no need to backdate the bill of lading; 2. The appellant Korea Exchange Bank could not prove with evidence that Koufuku Foods Company had participated in the act of backdating the bill of lading or had the subjective intent to backdate the bill of lading; the backdating of the bill of lading was a fraudulent act committed by the carrier to fulfill its promise to Koufuku Foods Company and was unrelated to Koufuku Foods Company; 3. Although Korea Exchange Bank claimed that due to the backdating of the bill of lading, the goods arrived late at the port, causing substantial damage to the L/C applicant, it did not submit corresponding evidence. For the L/C applicant, the backdating of the bill of lading in this case did not cause it actual damage. Therefore, although there was the fact of backdating the bill of lading in this case, there was no subjective malice on the part of the L/C beneficiary to commit fraud thereby. Koufuku Foods Company was not at fault for the backdating of the bill of lading, and it cannot be found to constitute L/C fraud; Korea Exchange Bank also cannot refuse to pay the L/C proceeds on this ground.

Article 106, paragraph 2 of the General Principles of Civil Law provides: "A citizen or legal person who, through fault, infringes upon state or collective property or infringes upon the property or person of another shall bear civil liability." In summary, the grounds of appeal of the appellant Korea Exchange Bank that the appellee Koufuku Foods Company had forged documents, backdated the bill of lading, and supplied goods with quality issues, and that the principle of L/C fraud exception should be applied to determine that it should not bear the responsibility for payment, were untenable. Regarding issues such as the discrepancy between the English name of the beneficiary on the negotiating documents and that on the L/C, and the backdating of the loading date on the bill of lading, Koufuku Foods Company was not subjectively at fault for fraud and could not bear civil liability for L/C fraud. The judgment of the first instance ordering Korea Exchange Bank to bear the obligation of payment under the L/C was not erroneous and should be affirmed. Accordingly, the Jiangsu High People's Court, in accordance with Article 153, paragraph 1, item (1) of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, rendered the following judgment on December 23, 2003:

Appeal dismissed; the original judgment is affirmed.

The case acceptance fee for the second instance of RMB 14,137 shall be borne by the appellant Korea Exchange Bank.
